This is a good question. I am not sure I have a good answer on this. One way to check the assumptions is to find the factor scores of latent variables and check the assumptions on the factor scores like multiple regressions. Here is the example code:

library(lavaan)
mod2 <- ' X  =~ x1 + x2 + x3
              M =~ x4 + x5 + x6
              Y   =~ x7 + x8 + x9
 Y ~ c*X + b*M
M ~ a*X
ab := a*b
total := c + (a*b)
'

fit2 <- sem(mod2, data=HolzingerSwineford1939)

summary(fit2, fit.measures=TRUE)

fscores <- predict(fit2)
fscores <- as.data.frame(fscores)

library(psych)
describe(fscores)

predM <- coef(fit2)["a"] * fscores$X
predY <- coef(fit2)["c"] * fscores$X + coef(fit2)["b"] * fscores$M
residM <- fscores$M - predM
residY <- fscores$Y - predY

# Residual plot to check the heteroscadasticity
plot(predM, residM)
plot(predY, residY)

# Check the skewness and kurtosis of residuals
describe(cbind(residM, residY))

For the multicollinearity, you can just run CFA and see the factor correlations of predictors. To check the tolerance, you can predict one predictors by other predictors and investigate R-square (i.e., 1 - tolerance).

On Tue, Sep 2, 2014 at 1:31 PM, <laura.m...@googlemail.com> wrote:

> Hi there!
>
> Does anybody know if it is possible to test several assumptions (e.g. homoscedasticy, multikollinearity, normal distribution of residuals)
> within a mediation model (with more than 1 mediator)?
>
> Thank you so much for your advice!
>
> Laura
