To follow up on Sunthud's response: this approach does not work for manifest mediation models because the current version of lav_predict <https://github.com/yrosseel/lavaan/blob/master/R/lav_predict.R> seems to be written specifically for factor scores. With manifest models it simply returns

Error in VETA[[g]] %*% t(LAMBDA[[g]]) : non-conformable arguments

So, using the example on the mediation tutorial page <http://lavaan.ugent.be/tutorial/mediation.html>, here's a (hacky) way of getting around this:

First, create the data:

set.seed(1234)
X <- rnorm(100)
M <- 0.5*X + rnorm(100)
Y <- 0.7*M + rnorm(100)
Data <- data.frame(X = X, Y = Y, M = M)

Then define the variable as pseudo-latent (i.e. single-indicator latent variables with a loading of 1 and a residual of 0)

latemod <- '
  lY =~ 1*Y
  Y ~~ 0*Y

  lM =~ 1*M
  M ~~ 0

  lX =~ 1*X
  X ~~ 0*X

  lM ~ lX
  lY ~ lX + lM'

latefit <- sem(latemod,Data)

If you want, you can also define the manifest version to ensure that the results are the same:
manimod <- '
  M ~ X
  Y ~ M + X'
manifit <- sem(manimod,Data)

Using the predicted values of the pseudo-latent variables you can then run the diagnostics that Sunthud suggested.

fscores <- predict(latefit)

