On 08/31/2014 03:06 AM, Enjoy Nicotine wrote:
> I would like to ask is: do I have to state in the cfa() that the items
> are ordered if I am going to use estimator=”WLSMV”  ?

It depends what you want. If you want to treat (some) items as ordinal,
you must either

1) declare the items are ordered in the data.frame
2) use the ordered= argument to declare them as ordered

If you do so, lavaan will treat them as ordinal, and use the WLSMV
estimator by default.

If you do not declare the items as ordinal, you can still use the WLSMV
estimator, but lavaan will treat all variables as continuous. Estimator
MLR is probably better suited for this.

> data are ordinal, or does the fact that lavaan gives me a warning
> message and the last eigenvalue is negative, suggest that it was wrong
> to free the residual errors of the four pairs of items?

Could be, but it is hard to tell without actually seeing the data and
the full R script.

In any case, with 7-point likert scales, I would treat them as
continuous as using estimator MLR.

Yves.

From u89c9fc822e34b78c at groups.google.com  Tue Sep 30 12:10:41 2014
From: u89c9fc822e34b78c at groups.google.com (Yves Rosseel)
Date: Tue, 30 Sep 2014 12:10:41 +0000
Subject: Re: group.partial argument in higher-order model
Message-ID: <LwTTywjgS9Y.aCrr7TDevH4J@lavaan.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <LwTTywjgS9Y.rjtZpgOUikwJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <LwTTywjgS9Y.rjtZpgOUikwJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/LwTTywjgS9Y/m/aCrr7TDevH4J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

> I want to fit a model so that everything is constrained but the
> second-order mean and variance. The second-order variance is released
> using the group.partial argument, but not the second-order mean
>
> second.2a.fit <- cfa(second.order.model, sample.cov=combined.cov,
> sample.mean=combined.mean,sample.nobs=combined.n,
> group.equal=c("loadings", "residuals","lv.variances",
> "intercepts","means"),group.partial=c("g~~g", "g~1"))

As a (late) follow-up: this is not so much a bug, it is a limitation of
the group.equal (and group.partial) arguments. They were not designed
with second-order factor models in mind.

The logic is as follows:

1) determine the free parameters in all groups
1b) if we fix the loadings + intercepts, we can also estimate the latent
means in all but the first group
1c) if we also force the latent means to be the same (group.equal
includes "means"), fix all latent means/intercepts to zero
2) which (free!) parameters are constrained to be equal across groups
(loadings, variances, ...)
3) use group.partial to override (otherwise free) parameters that were
constrained to be equal across groups

Because of 1c), the group.partial argument has no (longer) any effect on
the latent intercepts/means, because they are not recognized any longer
as free parameters.

The only way out (for now, 0.5-17) is to use the syntax, perhaps as follows:

fit <- cfa(c(second.order.model, 'verb + perf + mem ~ c(0,0)*1'),

sample.cov=combined.cov,
sample.mean=combined.mean,sample.nobs=combined.n,
group.equal=c("loadings", "residuals","lv.variances",

"intercepts"),group.partial=c("g~~g"))

Yves.

